    "audience": "creators",
    "collection": "industry-50",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Start with capacity instead of an empty calendar",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Count the working periods you can realistically use, including preparation, production, review and administration. An available afternoon does not automatically equal a finished piece. Look at recent work or time a small practice project to understand the steps involved. Choose a planning period short enough that you can see your commitments clearly. A two-week plan you can maintain is more useful than a full quarter of dates you keep moving.",
          "Reserve time for existing promises before adding new ideas. If another person must review the work, include their response time as a dependency rather than assuming immediate approval. Leave space for interruptions and corrections. The calendar should describe the work you intend to do within your capacity, not become a daily record of failing to meet an unrealistic publishing target."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Give each planned item a clear purpose",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Choose the question or need each item addresses. A profile update may explain your current service, an introduction may support applications and a short informational post may answer a repeated enquiry. Identify the intended audience and destination. Without those details, a calendar can fill with generic entries such as “make content” that still require all the important decisions when the work period begins.",
          "Keep an idea list separate from scheduled work. An idea can remain useful without having a deadline. Move it into the calendar when you know the purpose, the necessary inputs and the next action. If you repeatedly postpone the same item, examine what is missing. You may need permission, a clearer brief or a smaller version, rather than another date."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Track the stage that determines the next action",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Use a few understandable stages, such as planned, preparing, ready for review and ready to publish. Record the person responsible for the next step and where the working file lives. A calendar date alone cannot tell you whether an item needs recording or is waiting for approval. Avoid adding so many statuses that maintaining the system becomes a separate production job.",
          "In an example question-and-answer week, Monday could hold a review of recurring enquiries, Wednesday a draft of practical answers and Friday a final accuracy check. The draft depends on choosing questions you can answer without exposing private correspondence; publication depends on checking the answers. Link those tasks in the notes. If one slips, you can move the affected work deliberately rather than discovering the gap at publication time.",
          "Use a visible distinction between a target date and a confirmed external deadline. A personal profile update can often move, while a delivery promised to another person needs a conversation when timing changes. Put the contact action in the calendar too. This makes a delay something you manage, rather than a date that silently shifts while a collaborator continues to plan around the version they were originally given."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Batch compatible tasks without creating a backlog",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Group work when doing so removes repeated setup. You might review several captions together or prepare two introductions in the same quiet period. Keep enough time afterward to review and finish what you start. Recording a large collection can create a pile of unfinished editing if that stage has no capacity. Batch size should follow the whole workflow rather than the convenience of one step.",
          "Separate essential items from optional ones. If a client delivery and a personal profile update share the same week, decide the priority before an interruption forces the choice. Have a smaller version available only when it still serves the original purpose. Skipping an optional post can be a better decision than publishing work you have not checked or extending a working period beyond your limits."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Review the plan using what happened",
        "paragraphs": [
          "At the end of the planning period, compare estimates with actual work. Note where the delay occurred: obtaining input, making the piece, reviewing it or arranging publication. Keep the observation specific. “Caption review needed another session” helps the next estimate; “be more consistent” gives you nothing concrete to change. You do not need a complicated dashboard to learn from a handful of completed tasks.",
          "Carry forward only the work you still intend to do. Remove outdated ideas, close finished tasks and make the next calendar from the capacity you now have. If you publish availability, update that separately when commitments change. The working calendar can remain private and flexible. Use it to make reliable decisions about the next period rather than promising a public output schedule you have not yet shown you can maintain."
        ]
      }
    ],
